        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\n> ... allow automatically select the first element so I can just hit tab or enter to select without having to use the arrow keys ...\n\nThis is already possible and default 'menuone,noinsert,noselect' setting is designed for that. I personally use `<Tab>` for that with [this kind of custom mappings](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/3049adbafd08b769d0700624fe9b85fa4176c88c/doc/mini-completion.txt#L120-L128). They allow pressing `<Tab>` to select first completion item and keep typing.\n\nAlso a side note: 'mini.completion' uses built-in popup menu for which an intended way of navigating through completions is `<C-n>` (for \"next\") and `<C-p>` (for \"previous\"). So no need to use arrow keys.\n\n> ... automatically adding parentheses after a function is selected from the completion menu and placing the cursor inside.\n\nYes, I think @wenjinnn is correct here and this kind of functionality is meant to be implemented through snippets. That would (probably) require pressing another key to \"confirm\" selection, though. By default built-in completion uses `<C-y>` (for \"yes\") but that can be remapped.\n\nClosing as both already possible and tracked in another issue.",
